{
  "image_name": "aabc3130980b11f98cc6bd8aa4e5570a.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure combines a device layout with an energy-level picture to explain how an organic light-emitting device works. On the left, a layered OLED-like structure is shown on a transparent substrate with an ITO anode at the bottom and a metallic cathode on top. Between them are the hole-transporting layer, emitting layer, and electron-transporting layer. Positive and negative charges are injected from opposite electrodes and move into the central emitting region, where they meet and form excited states.\n\nThe right side summarizes what happens to those excitations. Charges first create higher excited singlet and triplet states, which then relax into lower singlet and triplet levels. From the singlet state, emission can occur directly as prompt fluorescence, while delayed fluorescence appears after intersystem crossing to triplet states and reverse intersystem crossing back to the singlet. The diagram also indicates non-radiative losses from both singlet and triplet states, and phosphorescence from the triplet manifold. Overall, the figure highlights the relationship between device architecture, charge recombination, and the competing radiative and non-radiative pathways that determine light emission efficiency.",
